---
import { links } from '../links';

const parts = [
  {
    title: 'Objects',
    body: 'The global state is a set of discrete objects rather than a tree. Each one carries an ID, a version, an owner, a replication factor, and up to 4 KB of content. Two transactions touching different objects can never collide, and when they touch the same one, a version check catches it where a lock would otherwise be needed.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Consensus',
    body: 'Ordering runs on a leaderless DAG. Every validator produces one vertex per round, linking to a stake supermajority of the round before, so a round advances on the group rather than on one proposer you have to wait for or bribe. Committing an anchor applies its whole causal history in an order that is a pure function of the hash links, identical on every node.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Pods',
    body: 'Backends are Rust compiled to WebAssembly, running in a sandbox whose entire interface is four host functions: declare gas, ask for the input size, read the input, write the output. A pod reaches no filesystem, network or clock, because the sandbox has no way to hand it one. Those four functions are the whole attack surface.',
  },
];
---

<section class="band" id="mechanics">
  <div class="wrap reveal">
    <div class="head">
      <div>
        <h2>Three pieces do most of the work.</h2>
      </div>
      <a class="jump" href={links.whitepaper} rel="noopener">Full whitepaper &#8627;</a>
    </div>

    <div class="grid grid--3">
      {
        parts.map((part) => (
          <article class="card">
            <h3>{part.title}</h3>
            <p>{part.body}</p>
          </article>
        ))
      }
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
